# Hardcore Mode — Engine-Side Design (permadeath → spectator game-over)

## Validation summary

The architecture you described checks out against the code. The single most important confirmed fact: **every lethal damage path funnels through exactly four arrow methods on `GameEngine`** — `applyDamage`, `applyEnvironmentalDamage`, `applyPoisonDamage`, `applyStarvationFloored`. Systems (`playerMotion`, `playerStats`, `mobAI`, `explosion`, `projectileAI`) only ever receive these as injected closures; none of them touch `state.isDead` directly except to *read* it as a gate. Only two of the four can ever be lethal (`applyDamage` and `applyEnvironmentalDamage`) — poison and starvation-floored call `applyNonLethalDamage`, which never sets `isDead`. So **there are exactly two interception points**, and the void/fall/explosion/arrow/mob/water/lava/drown paths all reach them. This is the clean seam to branch on.

---

## 1. State

### `GameState` additions (`lib/game/engine/state.ts`)
```ts
/** Hardcore world: forces hard+survival, locks both, permadeath. Immutable for the world's life. Persisted (save v10). */
hardcore: boolean;
/** True once a hardcore run has ended in death — drives spectator + Game Over. Persisted (save v10). */
gameOver: boolean;
```
Both are persisted (unlike `isDead`/`respawnTimer`, which stay session-only).

### `GameSnapshot` additions
```ts
/** True for a hardcore world — disables the pause-menu mode/difficulty switchers. */
hardcore: boolean;
/** True after a hardcore death — drives the Game Over screen (spectating the dead world). */
gameOver: boolean;
```
Add matching entries to `PRE_MOUNT_SNAPSHOT` in `useMinecraftGame.ts` (`hardcore: false, gameOver: false`) and to `buildSnapshot()`.

### Constructor init logic (`GameEngine.ts`)

Resolve `hardcore` first, because it overrides the mode/difficulty resolution that already exists:

```ts
const hardcore = save ? restoreHardcore(save) : (options.hardcore ?? false);
const gameOver = save ? restoreGameOver(save) : false;

// Precedence: hardcore forces hard+survival. A restored gameOver boots straight
// into spectator so the dead world is roamable, not playable.
const difficulty = hardcore ? "hard" : (save ? restoreDifficulty(save) : (options.difficulty ?? "normal"));
const gameMode = gameOver ? "spectator"
  : hardcore ? "survival"
  : (save ? restoreGameMode(save) : (options.gameMode ?? "survival"));
```

Then in the state literal: `hardcore`, `gameOver`, and the existing `isFlying: gameMode === "spectator"` already yields `isFlying: true` when `gameOver` forced spectator — no extra wiring needed there.

**Key subtlety — the constructor's stuck-check already does the right thing.** Lines 242-247 skip `forceUnstuck()` when `isNoclip(gameMode)` is true. Because a restored `gameOver` resolves `gameMode` to `"spectator"`, the dead-world player is never yanked out of terrain on load. That branch needs no change.

**Do NOT restore `isDead`/`respawnTimer`** — they remain unserialized. A reloaded dead world has `isDead === false` (fresh) and `gameOver === true`, which is exactly the resume state: spectator free-cam, no respawn countdown.

Add `hardcore?: boolean` to `GameEngineOptions`.

