## 3. The locks

Guard the two switch methods at their top, so both the command path and any future caller no-op under hardcore:

```ts
private switchGameMode(next: GameMode): void {
  const state = this.state;
  if (state.hardcore) return;   // locked to survival (or spectator after gameOver)
  if (!isGameMode(next) || next === state.gameMode || state.isDead) return;
  ...
}

private switchDifficulty(next: Difficulty): void {
  const state = this.state;
  if (state.hardcore) return;   // locked to hard
  if (!isDifficulty(next) || next === state.difficulty) return;
  ...
}
```

This is the correct layer: `dispatch`'s `setGameMode`/`setDifficulty` cases call these, so the commands become no-ops. `triggerGameOver()` sets `state.gameMode` **directly**, never through `switchGameMode`, so the engine's own transition still works while the player-facing command is blocked. Difficulty is set to `"hard"` once in the constructor and never mutated (the only mutation site is `switchDifficulty`, now guarded), so it stays `"hard"` for the world's life.

The UI side (pause-menu switchers disabled) reads `snapshot.hardcore`; that's outside engine scope but the snapshot field carries it.

